---
// src/pages/publications/sharps_rifle.astro
// Short piece: no endnotes, no cross-references, one unnumbered figure, and
// no citation_* journal metadata. Same pattern as albert_pike.
import RootLayout from '../../layouts/RootLayout.astro';
import ScholarlyMeta from '../../components/ScholarlyMeta.astro';
import PublicationHeader from '../../components/PublicationHeader.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import CiteThis from '../../components/CiteThis.astro';
import { CITATION, FIGURES } from '../../data/publications/sharps-rifle';

const c = CITATION;

// The opening pull-quote doubles as the standfirst, so it feeds the meta
// description and the link preview rather than being repeated as an abstract.
const SUMMARY =
  'Dating to 1848, the Sharps rifle and its patented breech-loading, falling block design was a ' +
  'go-to firearm for snipers and long-distance shooters due to its accuracy and ease of use. ' +
  'Christian Sharps left the enterprise in the 1850s, but the Sharps Rifle Company carried on ' +
  'using his name to market rifles for the next several decades.';
---

<RootLayout
  title={`${c.title} | ${c.authors[0]}`}
  description={SUMMARY}
  image="/images/judia_buffalo_stand.jpg"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <ScholarlyMeta citation={c} />
  </Fragment>

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto py-6 text-iron" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/Article">

    <!-- Breadcrumb Anchor Navigation -->
    <div class="mb-8 flex justify-between items-center">
      <p>
        <a href="/publications" class="text-sm font-serif text-sage hover:text-caprock transition-colors lowercase tracking-wide">
          &larr; return to repository index
        </a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/cv" class="text-sm font-serif text-sage hover:text-caprock transition-colors lowercase tracking-wide">
          return to cv &rarr;
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>

    <PublicationHeader citation={c} eyebrow="Caprock Chronicles Feature" />

    <!-- Article Body -->
    <div class="prose-article max-w-3xl mx-auto font-serif text-[17px] leading-[1.75] text-iron/90 space-y-6" itemprop="articleBody">

      <blockquote class="border-l-2 border-caprock pl-5 italic text-iron/70 text-lg leading-relaxed">
        Several prominent names are associated with American firearm development during the 19th century: Oliver Winchester, Eliphalet Remington, Jacob and Samuel Hawken, Melchior Fordney, Christian Sharps, etc. A lesser known of these gunsmiths is Christian Sharps. Dating to 1848, the Sharps rifle and its patented breech-loading, falling block design was a go-to firearm for snipers and long-distance shooters due to its accuracy and ease of use. Christian Sharps left the enterprise in the 1850s and the company moved to Connecticut, but the Sharps Rifle Company carried on using his name to market rifles for the next several decades.
      </blockquote>

      <Figure set={FIGURES} id="buffalo-stand" numbered={false} />

      <p>
        Texas Senator Thomas Jefferson Rusk touted the new style rifle in an 1848 newspaper article noting that the rifle was &ldquo;the most convenient and fatal weapon ever used.&rdquo; This early model was capable of carrying 50 percussion caps at a time, which made it possible for a rifleman to shoot at a much more rapid pace than previously possible. It was the most popular carbine rifle during the Civil War, and it saw use for a significant amount of time following the war.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Model 1874 Sharps, which actually entered production in 1871, introduced the capability of the rifle to be chambered in a variety of calibers. The most prominent of these cartridges was the .45-70 (pronounced &ldquo;forty five seventy&rdquo;), but common chamberings in 45 caliber included .45-70, .45-90, .45-110, and even a .45-120 offering that came into production in the late 1870s or early 1880s. 40, 44 and 50 caliber options were also available, along with options for barrel length.
      </p>

      <p>
        Christian Sharps died in 1874 having left the company two decades prior, but the impact of his rifle perhaps was just becoming fully realized at his death with the advent of the Model 1874 version of the rifle. The 1870s saw the decimation of American buffalo herds on the Great Plains, one of which was prevalent on the southern plains of Texas. Buffalo hunters flooded the region in the 1870s influenced by forces in the eastern United States that desired not only buffalo pelts but also the forced submission of Native Americans to relocate to reservations.
      </p>

      <p>
        Written accounts of buffalo hunting on the plains consistently mention the abundant use of Sharps rifles. One such account is that of John R. Cook in his book <em>The Border and the Buffalo</em>. Cook spent a significant amount of time in Texas in the mid-1870s hunting buffalo in areas ranging between as far north as Fort Elliott in the eastern Texas panhandle to Fort Griffin and areas near the Double Mountain Fork Brazos River. Cook is most well-known for his recollection of buffalo hunter and United States Army skirmishes with the Comanche in 1877.
      </p>

      <p>
        Cook mentions possessing a Creedmoor 45 Sharps that he purchased at Fort Elliott in 1876, but he also mentions other hunters having Sharps rifles. Marshall Sewall, the hunter who was killed on Deep Creek in Scurry County in February 1877, also carried a variant of the rifle that fell into the hands of the marauding Comanche. Cook notes that the Comanche felt that this gun was cursed due to injuries and deaths that befell tribe members who were known to have used that particular gun in the following weeks. They eventually buried the gun in some sandhills, having adorned the firearm with the scalps they took from Sewall in February.
      </p>

      <p>
        One of the defining features of later Sharps rifles was the advent of long-distance, ladder-style sights, often called Creedmoor, Soule, Tang, or Vernier sights, that allowed shooters to adjust for elevation and windage when shooting at targets well over 400 yards or more. Cook mentions these Creedmoor sights and his ability to shoot buffalo 300 yards distant. The use of these sights allowed buffalo hunters on the plains to remain comfortable distances away from the animals that were prone to launching into stampedes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Texas Tech University&rsquo;s Southwest Collection/Special Collections Library is home to a small, digitized collection of letters sent to the Sharps Rifle Company in Hartford, Connecticut. The bulk of these letters originated from Texas and largely consist of orders for custom rifles, bullet molds, or parts to service or repair guns. Frank E. Conrad, a retailer from Fort Griffin, sent the Sharps company a letter in February 1880 requesting six custom 40-caliber rifles with American Walnut stocks, 26 inch barrels, and single triggers. Even on the frontier of Texas, the Sharps Rifle Company&rsquo;s decades-long reputation made them a reliable source for mail-order rifle customers across the nation, a service that brought superior products to the frontier on a large scale.
      </p>

      <p>
        The company went out of business in the early 1880s, but the legacy of the rifle company persists with modern replicas that are available through a variety of gunsmiths with even more custom options available than were available in the 19th century. These rifles remain a mainstay at shooting competitions and gun ranges.
      </p>

    </div>

    <CiteThis citation={c} />

  </article>
</RootLayout>

<style is:global>
  /* One knob for every in-page jump, so nothing lands under the sticky header. */
  :root {
    --anchor-offset: 7.5rem;
  }

  .versions .version-link {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.6rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-haze, #ddd8cf);
    border-radius: 2px;
    color: rgb(from var(--color-iron, #3a3733) r g b / 0.6);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease, border-color 150ms ease;
  }
  .versions a.version-link:hover,
  .versions a.version-link:focus-visible {
    color: #9c5b3f;
    border-color: #9c5b3f;
  }
  .versions .version-link--current {
    color: #9c5b3f;
    border-color: #9c5b3f;
    background: rgb(from #9c5b3f r g b / 0.06);
  }

  @media print {
    :root {
      --anchor-offset: 0;
    }
    nav,
    .versions,
    a[href="/publications"],
    a[download] {
      display: none !important;
    }
    .prose-article {
      max-width: none;
      font-size: 10.5pt;
      line-height: 1.5;
      color: #000;
    }
    h1 {
      font-size: 18pt;
    }
    .prose-article a[href^="http"]::after {
      content: " (" attr(href) ")";
      font-size: 8pt;
      word-break: break-all;
    }
    p {
      orphans: 3;
      widows: 3;
    }
  }
</style>